<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The petitioners and the first respondent had been, at different points, partners of an LLP constituted under an agreement of October 2019, supplemented twice in 2020. The quarrels were classically internal: a claim to accounts stretching back years, the first respondent&rsquo;s contested expulsion from the LLP, and a disputed &ldquo;settlement&rdquo; of 7 December 2025 that one petitioner never signed and which contained blanks. The first petitioner invoked arbitration by notice dated 17 June 2025; when no tribunal emerged, a petition under Section 11(6) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act followed.</p>
<p>The structural problem: none of the three agreements contained an arbitration clause, and Clause 33 conferred jurisdiction on the courts at Mumbai.</p>

<h2>How the statute filled the gap</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 23(4), LLP Act.</strong> Where the LLP agreement does not provide for a matter, the First Schedule governs the mutual rights and duties of the partners. Silence, in LLP law, is not a vacuum — it is a default setting.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Entry 14, First Schedule.</strong> Disputes between partners arising out of the LLP agreement which cannot be resolved in terms of the agreement shall be referred to arbitration under the 1996 Act. The court stressed the two statutory conditions — &ldquo;these twin conditions have to be satisfied&rdquo; — and found both met: the disputes arose out of the agreement, and the agreement offered no internal mechanism to resolve them.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 21 notice by one claimant sufficed.</strong> The invocation notice issued by the first petitioner alone was no defect; the notice&rsquo;s function is to fix the commencement of arbitration and stop limitation, not to assemble every potential party.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appointment.</strong> A former judge of the Bombay High Court was appointed sole arbitrator, with the seat at Mumbai and costs shared equally, subject to the award.</div>
</div>

<h2>Objections raised, and where they went</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Objection</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
  <tr><td>No arbitration clause in the LLP agreement</td><td>Irrelevant — Entry 14 of the First Schedule applies by force of Section 23(4) where the agreement is silent.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Clause 33 confers exclusive jurisdiction on Mumbai courts</td><td>Governs court proceedings under the Arbitration Act; does not exclude arbitration. Seat fixed at Mumbai.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Disputes settled — accord and satisfaction</td><td>&ldquo;Even if there is accord/satisfaction it will not bar an arbitration&rdquo; — especially where the settlement&rsquo;s validity is itself disputed.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Non-signatory seeking impleadment</td><td>Left to the tribunal: it &ldquo;would be full empowered to examine this issue in the first instance.&rdquo;</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What this means for LLPs and their partners</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Audit your LLP agreement.</strong> If it is silent on dispute resolution, arbitration is already your forum — whether the partners know it or not. Firms that would prefer court litigation must say so expressly, to the extent the Act permits departure from the Schedule.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Statutory arbitration is real arbitration.</strong> The reference, once made, proceeds under the 1996 Act like any contractual arbitration — interim measures, disclosure, award and challenge included.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Settlement documents need execution discipline.</strong> A half-signed settlement with blanks resolved nothing and barred nothing; it merely added a dispute. Accord-and-satisfaction defences belong to the tribunal, not the Section 11 court.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Expulsion fights go to arbitrators.</strong> Contested removals of partners — among the commonest LLP disputes — fall squarely within Entry 14 when the agreement provides no resolution mechanism of its own.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">The Section 11 court&rsquo;s role remains confined to a prima facie view; everything else — accounts, expulsion, the December 2025 settlement, the non-signatory&rsquo;s position — now belongs to the sole arbitrator. For Delhi practitioners, the decision is a useful reminder that the LLP Act&rsquo;s First Schedule operates identically everywhere: the arbitration clause many LLPs never drafted has been sitting in the statute since 2008.</div>
